    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d422">

                <pb facs="0361" n="355" sameAs="frus:frus1939v04/pg355"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">393.112/19: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Chargé in Japan</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Dooman</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1939-07-20T18:00:00-04:00">July 20, 1939—6 p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>207. Shanghai’s 613, July 17, 7 p.m.<anchor n="22" xml:id="d422-f1"/> Please
                    bring this matter to the attention of the Minister for Foreign Affairs and
                    supplement the representations made at Shanghai by pointing out that the
                    American Government takes a serious view of this and similar incidents, for
                    which there appears to be no warrant whatsoever, and that this Government is
                    becoming increasingly apprehensive with regard to the cumulative effect which
                    such incidents as these may have upon public opinion in this country.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d422-f1" n="22" xml:id="d422-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed; it reported an unprovoked assault upon two American missionaries by
                    a Japanese consular police officer at Hangchow.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
